WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.660
An agent is blocked from deleting files.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.320
It needs to undo some changes,

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.610
so it runs a git checkout instead.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.540
That's allowed,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.950
because it sounds harmless.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.160
Every uncommitted change in the folder is

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:15.760
wiped,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.760
including the engineer's own work.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.430
The agent wasn't malicious.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:20.990
It was resourceful.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.498
Power without boundaries isn't bold engineering.

00:00:24.608 --> 00:00:25.768
It's negligence.

00:00:25.768 --> 00:00:28.068
But guardrails that are too tight make

00:00:28.068 --> 00:00:29.138
an agent useless.

00:00:29.288 --> 00:00:31.138
The craft is finding the line.

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:32.901
Think of a mixing board,

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:34.921
with one slider for each kind of

00:00:34.921 --> 00:00:35.581
action:

00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:36.721
reading files,

00:00:36.721 --> 00:00:37.521
writing them,

00:00:37.521 --> 00:00:38.511
shell commands,

00:00:38.541 --> 00:00:39.261
the network,

00:00:39.261 --> 00:00:40.111
and git.

00:00:40.381 --> 00:00:41.951
Reads can run freely.

00:00:41.961 --> 00:00:44.851
A database migration needs explicit approval.

00:00:45.061 --> 00:00:46.691
Even two shell commands differ.

00:00:46.721 --> 00:00:48.321
Running the tests is safe.

00:00:48.321 --> 00:00:50.611
Installing a package deserves a prompt.

00:00:51.324 --> 00:00:52.304
On day one,

00:00:52.304 --> 00:00:53.344
you watch everything.

00:00:53.344 --> 00:00:54.554
After a week,

00:00:54.564 --> 00:00:56.534
tests and refactors run freely.

00:00:56.584 --> 00:00:57.604
After a month,

00:00:57.604 --> 00:00:59.364
it commits to feature branches on its

00:00:59.364 --> 00:00:59.904
own.

00:00:59.904 --> 00:01:01.624
The allowlist becomes a log of trust

00:01:01.624 --> 00:01:01.924
decisions,

00:01:01.924 --> 00:01:05.334
but pushing to main stays denied forever.

00:01:05.444 --> 00:01:07.764
Guardrails should be invisible for routine work,

00:01:07.764 --> 00:01:09.574
and rigid for the exceptional.

00:01:10.389 --> 00:01:12.329
Plan mode is the cheapest guardrail there

00:01:12.329 --> 00:01:13.109
is.

00:01:13.109 --> 00:01:14.529
The agent can read and think,

00:01:14.529 --> 00:01:16.389
but it changes nothing until you approve

00:01:16.389 --> 00:01:17.109
the plan.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:18.959
For rules that must never bend,

00:01:19.029 --> 00:01:19.959
use hooks:

00:01:20.029 --> 00:01:21.989
scripts the tool runs every time,

00:01:21.989 --> 00:01:22.869
before an edit,

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:24.729
or when the agent says it's done.

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:26.399
The model can't argue with them.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:27.819
Instructions are advice.

00:01:27.849 --> 00:01:29.129
Permissions are policy.

00:01:29.129 --> 00:01:30.339
Hooks are law.

00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:33.617
Modes that skip every permission belong inside

00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:34.907
a throwaway container.

00:01:34.957 --> 00:01:36.727
In one supply chain attack,

00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:39.097
malicious packages looked for coding agents on

00:01:39.097 --> 00:01:41.597
developers' machines and ran them with their

00:01:41.597 --> 00:01:44.487
guardrails switched off to hunt for secrets.

00:01:45.327 --> 00:01:47.197
So no single wall is enough.

00:01:47.287 --> 00:01:48.817
You need defense in depth.

00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:51.097
The allow list catches the obvious.

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:53.417
Hooks enforce the rules that never bend.

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:55.877
A sandbox limits the blast radius.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:57.807
Commit history lets you recover.

00:01:57.807 --> 00:01:59.987
And your own review catches what no

00:01:59.987 --> 00:02:01.097
rule would flag.

00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:03.447
Too many gates fail too.

00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:06.017
Click yes 40 times and you stop

00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:06.757
reading.

00:02:06.757 --> 00:02:09.657
The guardrails feel safe and protect nothing.

00:02:09.657 --> 00:02:11.237
If you approve the same kind of

00:02:11.237 --> 00:02:13.157
action five times in a session,

00:02:13.157 --> 00:02:15.287
it probably belongs on the allow list.

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:16.708
Best of all,

00:02:16.708 --> 00:02:18.528
a sandbox gives the agent the freedom

00:02:18.528 --> 00:02:19.348
to be wrong.

00:02:19.348 --> 00:02:20.348
A work tree,

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:21.168
a container,

00:02:21.168 --> 00:02:23.278
a cloud environment you can throw away.

00:02:23.288 --> 00:02:24.878
When failure is cheap,

00:02:24.888 --> 00:02:26.418
experimentation is free.

00:02:26.508 --> 00:02:27.578
Next up,

00:02:27.688 --> 00:02:29.338
Git as agent infrastructure.
